Title: Nightfall Over Salford
Credit: Written by
Author: The Stripboard Project
Draft date: 2026-08-05
Contact: A sample screenplay. Original, and written to exercise a scheduler.

Notes: Fourteen scenes across nine locations, day and night, with a cast that includes
two leads carrying most of the picture and four supporting parts appearing once or twice.
That distribution is the point: it is what a real call sheet looks like, and it is what
makes cast utilisation, company moves and night/day banking worth solving.

INT. SALFORD SORTING OFFICE - NIGHT

Conveyor belts run empty under sodium light. MAEVE OKONKWO, 40s, walks the line with a
torch, checking seals she already checked an hour ago.

MAEVE
Third night this week the count's wrong.

She stops at a sack that has been opened and retied badly.

MAEVE (CONT'D)
And that's not a machine doing that.

EXT. SALFORD QUAYS - NIGHT

Maeve crosses the water on the swing bridge. Behind her, a figure keeps pace and stops
when she stops.

INT. MAEVE'S FLAT - NIGHT

Maeve puts the chain on the door. She lays four envelopes on the kitchen table, all
addressed in the same hand.

MAEVE
Same hand. Different postmarks.

INT. GREATER MANCHESTER POLICE - CENTRAL - DAY

DI TOMÁS REYES, 50s, tired in the way that does not improve with sleep, reads the
envelopes through an evidence bag while Maeve stands rather than sits.

REYES
You opened them.

MAEVE
I sorted them. There's a difference, and you'd know it if you'd ever done the job.

REYES
Four letters isn't a case.

MAEVE
It is if the fifth one comes.

INT. GREATER MANCHESTER POLICE - EVIDENCE STORE - DAY

Reyes signs the bag in. The CLERK, 20s, does not look up from a paperback.

CLERK
You want it flagged?

REYES
I want it looked at by someone who cares. Flag it anyway.

EXT. ORDSALL PARK - DAY

Reyes walks with DR. PRIYA NAIR, a document examiner, who holds a photocopy up to the
grey sky.

NAIR
Same pen. Same pressure. Whoever wrote these wrote them in one sitting, then posted them
over three weeks from three different towns.

REYES
Which means they planned the waiting.

NAIR
Which means the waiting is the message.

INT. SALFORD SORTING OFFICE - DAY

Daylight makes the place ordinary. Maeve shows Reyes the belt, the sacks, the door that
does not lock.

MAEVE
Anyone with a lanyard walks in here. I've said so in writing four times.

REYES
Show me the writing.

INT. SORTING OFFICE - MANAGER'S OFFICE - DAY

DEREK HALLIWELL, 60s, manager, finds the complaints in a drawer he opens too quickly to
have been searching.

HALLIWELL
They were logged. Logged isn't the same as ignored.

REYES
It's the same to the person the letters are about.

EXT. SALFORD QUAYS - DAY

Reyes stands where the figure stood the night Maeve was followed. He looks back at the
bridge and sees exactly what a follower would see.

INT. MAEVE'S FLAT - NIGHT

The fifth envelope is on the mat, unposted. No stamp. Maeve does not pick it up. She
photographs it where it lies, and only then calls.

EXT. MANCHESTER SHIP CANAL - NIGHT

Torch beams cross black water. Reyes and Maeve follow the towpath to a maintenance hut
with a light on inside.

INT. CANAL MAINTENANCE HUT - NIGHT

Four hundred envelopes, sorted into towns. A kettle, still warm. Halliwell's lanyard on
a nail.

MAEVE
He wasn't hiding them. He was rehearsing.

INT. GREATER MANCHESTER POLICE - INTERVIEW ROOM - DAY

Halliwell sits with his hands flat. He answers every question except the one he was asked.

HALLIWELL
Thirty-one years I ran that floor. Then they sent a woman who counts.

REYES
That's not a reason. That's a grievance with a stamp on it.

EXT. SALFORD SORTING OFFICE - DAY

Maeve unlocks the door herself. The belts start. She checks the seals once, and only once,
and goes home while it is still light.
